WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.621
The lab's gh-runs tool lists a repo's recent GitHub Actions runs, and exits 1 when one of them failed.

2
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:13.815
It asks with one GET request to the GitHub API.

3
00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:16.543
Let's follow that request through the code.

4
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.651
It starts in a requests Session.

5
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:23.479
The session keeps the connection open, and sends the same headers with every call.

6
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:32.186
The media type GitHub recommends, a pinned API version, and the token, read from the GITHUB_TOKEN environment variable.

7
00:00:32.186 --> 00:00:33.612
It is never printed.

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.276
Always pass a timeout.

9
00:00:35.276 --> 00:00:40.652
Requests has no default, so a server that never answers hangs your script forever.

10
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:47.450
Under the hood, requests sends every call through urllib3, a lower-level HTTP library.

11
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.053
The lab gives the session a Retry.

12
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:53.004
Then the request crosses the network to GitHub.

13
00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:54.416
GitHub answers.

14
00:00:54.416 --> 00:01:02.143
Call raise_for_status first: a 404 or a 500 is not an exception in requests until you ask for one.

15
00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:04.667
Then read the body with .json().

16
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.019
Now GitHub has a bad moment, and answers 502.

17
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:11.159
Asking again can help here.

18
00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:13.656
So Retry waits, and sends it again.

19
00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:18.149
With a backoff factor of 1, it waits 0, then 2, then 4 seconds.

20
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:26.269
The lab retries only when asking again can help: a 429, or a 500, 502, 503 or 504.

21
00:01:26.269 --> 00:01:32.823
A 404 or a 401 is never retried, because the answer will not change.

22
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.185
And only for GET.

23
00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:39.063
A repeated GET is harmless, while a repeated POST may start two deploys.

24
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:41.867
So how do you test the 502 case?

25
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:44.548
You cannot make GitHub fail on purpose.

26
00:01:44.548 --> 00:01:49.841
And tests that call the real service are slow, flaky, and need secrets in CI.

27
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:53.240
Replace the service, and keep your code as it is.

28
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:58.485
The responses library replaces the step where requests hands data to the network.

29
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.282
The test registers canned answers, in order.

30
00:02:02.282 --> 00:02:03.748
A 502, then a 200.

31
00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:05.405
Run the same code.

32
00:02:05.405 --> 00:02:07.642
The first answer is the 502.

33
00:02:07.642 --> 00:02:10.439
Retry asks again, and gets the 200.

34
00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:12.676
That proves the retry works.

35
00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:16.352
The test sets backoff=0, so it does not sleep.

36
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:21.102
A request to any URL you did not register raises ConnectionError.

37
00:02:21.102 --> 00:02:24.452
So a test cannot reach the internet by accident.

38
00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.244
AWS works the same way.

39
00:02:27.244 --> 00:02:36.237
boto3 turns each method call into an HTTPS request to an AWS API, signed with your credentials.

40
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:45.306
Inside mock_aws, moto catches each request just before it would be sent, and answers it from an in-memory fake AWS.

41
00:02:45.306 --> 00:02:48.149
That fake starts empty for each test.

42
00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.260
One rule from the moto docs.

43
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.235
Start the mock before you create any client.

44
00:02:53.235 --> 00:02:57.630
A client made earlier, for example at import time, is not mocked.

45
00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.620
It can reach real AWS, with whatever keys your shell holds.

46
00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:05.039
And moto is a model of AWS, not AWS.

47
00:03:05.039 --> 00:03:08.721
It does not check IAM permissions unless you ask.

48
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:13.831
So run a new check once against a real account, before you trust it.

49
00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:16.125
So the code never changes.

50
00:03:16.125 --> 00:03:18.597
In a run it talks to the real service.

51
00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:23.605
Under pytest, the far end is fake, and you can make it fail any way you like.
